REGIONAL REGULATION OF MAGETAN REGENCY

NUMBER 2 OF 2016

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF MAGETAN,

Considering:

a. that violence against women and children is an act that violates the rights and dignity of women and children as human beings;
b. that efforts to protect women and children victims of violence in the Region have not been optimal, thus requiring institutional strengthening and regulations that can guarantee its implementation in accordance with applicable provisions;
c. that based on Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015, women's empowerment and child protection are governmental affairs that must be implemented by Regional Government;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 12 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions Within the Province of East Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1950 Number 41) as amended by Law Number 2 of 1965 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1965 Number 19, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2730);
3. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette Number 3143);
4. Law Number 20 of 1999 concerning Ratification of ILO Convention No. 138 Concerning Minimum Age for Admission to Employment (ILO Convention concerning Minimum Age for Admission to Work) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 56, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3835);
5. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 75, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3854);
6. Law Number 1 of 2000 concerning Ratification of ILO Convention No. 182 Concerning The Prohibition and Immediate Action for The Elimination of The Worst Forms of Child Labour (ILO Convention No. 182 concerning Prohibition and Immediate Action for the Elimination of the Worst Forms of Child Labor) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2000 Number 30, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3941);
7. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
8. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
9. Law 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
10. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
11. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning Formation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
12. Law Number 11 of 2012 concerning the Juvenile Criminal Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 153 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);
13. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
14. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation of Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
15. Presidential Decree Number 36 of 1990 concerning the Convention on the Rights of the Child;
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 2 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for Implementation of Women's Protection;
17. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 3 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for Implementation of Child Protection;
18. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 2 of 2009 concerning Policy on Child-Friendly Regencies/Cities;
19. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1 of 2010 Concerning Minimum Service Standards for Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
20. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection of the Republic of Indonesia Number 05 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers;
21. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Handling Child Victims of Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 42);
22. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection of the Republic of Indonesia Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 42);
23. Regulation of the Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 6 of 2015 concerning the System for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 615);
24 Regional Regulation of East Java Province Number 16 of 2012 concerning Implementation of Protection for Women and Children Victims of Violence (Regional Gazette of East Java Province of 2013 Number 3 Series D);

With Joint Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF MAGETAN REGENCY

and

REGENT OF MAGETAN

DECIDES:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

Part Two

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

The implementation of protection for women and child victims of violence is based on:
a. respect and fulfillment of the rights of victims;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of victims; and
e. empowerment.

Article 3

This Regional Regulation aims to:
a. prevent all forms of violence against women and children;
b. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
c. provide services to women and child victims of acts of violence;
d. provide protection to reporters and witnesses; and
e. carry out empowerment of women and fulfillment of the rights of child victims of violence.

CHAPTER II

FORMS OF VIOLENCE

Article 4

Forms of violence include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. exploitation; and/or
f. other violence.

Article 6

1. The Regional Government is responsible for implementing efforts to prevent the occurrence of violence and protection of victims of violence.
2. Prevention of the occurrence of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) in the form of:
a. collecting data and information about women and children and related laws and regulations;
b. conducting education on anti-violence values; and
c. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the implementation of prevention and protection of victims of violence.
3. Protection of victims of violence as referred to (3) in paragraph (1) in the form of:
a. establishing and facilitating the implementation of integrated service institutions for victims by involving community elements;
b. facilitating accompaniment, legal aid and legal services according to the needs of victims;
c. providing housing both safe houses and alternative housing along with handling mechanisms, psychosocial and spiritual services;
d. conducting continuous handling up to the rehabilitation and social reintegration stage;
e. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection for victims of violence; and
f. encouraging community awareness of the importance of protection for victims of violence.
4. In order to carry out the responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government establishes programs and action activities for the protection of women and children in one Regional Action Plan for the Protection of Women and Child Victims of Violence by referring to the Regional Action Plan for the Protection of Women and Child Victims of Violence of East Java Province.
5. The Regional Action Plan for the Protection of Women (5) and Child Victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (2) is established for a period of 3 (three) years by Regent's Decree.

Article 9

1. Efforts to prevent violence against women and children are carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government and coordinated by regional apparatus organizations that administer affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.
2. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented by:
a. conducting guidance towards harmonious household integrity;
b. forming work networks in efforts to prevent violence;
c. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
d. forming a violence prevention system;
e. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of women and child victims of violence; and
f. providing critical education regarding the rights of women and children for the community.

Article 10

In addition to the Regional Government, prevention efforts as referred to in Article 9 paragraph (1) are carried out by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. educational institutions; and/or
d. non-governmental organizations.

Article 11

The provision of services to victims is carried out with:
a. speed;
b. safety and comfort;
c. a sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easy accessibility;
f. no charges; and
g. guaranteed confidentiality.

Article 12

Forms of services to victims include:
a. complaint, consultation, and counseling services;
b. assistance services;
c. health services;
d. social rehabilitation services;
e. legal services;
f. social reintegration services; and
g. economic independence services.

CHAPTER VI

VICTIMS' RIGHTS

Article 21

1. In the implementation of protection of women and child victims of violence, every victim receives the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity respected as a human being;
b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality of their identity;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to complaint handling;
h. the right to obtain facilitation in the judicial process; and/or
i. the right to assistance.
2. Children who become victims of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in paragraph (1), also receive special rights, as follows:
a. the right to respect for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to receive spiritual guidance;
d. the right to equal protection;
e. the right to be free from various stigma; and/or
f. the right to obtain freedom.

CHAPTER llII

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 22

1. The community participates in the implementation of protection of victims.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is in the form of:
a.forming family partners at the village/sub-district level;
b.forming women and child protection units within community organizations;
c. conducting independent socialization of women's and children's rights;
d. providing first aid to victims; and
e. reporting to the competent authorities if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
